[[build-collect]]
=== Collect, fold, parse and render

[abstract]
A command's docstring can attach data-flow directives to it: `@parse` reads
an argument from a file in a foreign format, `@collect` streams a command's
output instead of returning it whole, and output actions (`@render`, `@with`,
with `@fold`) turn that output into other results, each written to its own
destination. The chapters on <<arguments>>, <<streaming-output>> and
<<output-actions>> show how to use them; this section specifies where each
step runs, what is held in memory, and how several outputs are produced from
one run.

**Everything is compiled into ordinary commands.** The compiler lowers each
directive into Morloc code and, where needed, into extra commands in the
manifest that the user never names: a *parse entry* per command with parsed
arguments, an *action entry* per output action, and a *replay entry* per
action for multi-output runs. The nexus only chooses which entries to run and
where their output goes; all user-level work (parsing, folding, formatting)
runs in pools.

**`@parse`.** An argument declares named formats, each with file extensions.
The nexus picks a format by looking only at the argument's text, never the
file's contents:

. A `name:` prefix selects that format (`morloc:` reads an ordinary Morloc
  value).
. Otherwise the longest matching extension wins, ignoring case.
. Otherwise the argument is read as an ordinary value. `-` is standard input,
  which at most one argument may claim.

The parse entry then calls the format's handler in a pool with the path. A
plain argument's handler returns the value, and every such argument is parsed
before the command body starts. An `IStream` argument's handler feeds batches
to the command as it runs. An `IFile` argument is first written to a stream
file in a temporary *stage* directory and opened from there. A handler's
failure names the argument, format and path, and ends the run with status 1.

**`@collect`.** `@collect producer` gives the producer a sink and streams
whatever it passes to the sink to the command's output, batch by batch. The
batches travel through shared memory to the nexus, which writes each in the
requested format as it arrives. Nothing is gathered: memory use does not
grow with the output.

**Output actions** consume what the command collects. Each runs as its own
entry, a copy of the command whose sink is replaced:

[cols="1,4", options="header"]
|===
| Kind | Sink, and what the handler receives
| gather | Writes every batch to a temporary stream file. The handler receives the whole list, or an `IFile` handle to read it at random without loading it.
| `@stream` | Calls the handler on each batch inside the sink. Memory stays constant.
| `@fold` | Folds each batch into an accumulator with `@fold`, starting from `@init`. Each thread keeps its own accumulator; at the end they are merged with `@combine` in the order the threads first folded, and the handler receives the result.
|===

A `@render` handler returns bytes (`Str`, `[Str]`, `Vector U8` or a list of
`Vector U8`), which are written verbatim, ignoring `-f`. A `@with` handler
returns a typed value, which is written in the `-f` format.

**One output.** When only standard output is wanted, the nexus runs the
action's entry directly (or the command itself) and writes its result.

**Several outputs.** When an action writes to a file, or a command has more
than one action, the run is *staged*:

[mermaid]
....
flowchart LR
    S[stage: run the command once] -->|saved output| R1[replay action 1]
    S -->|saved output| R2[replay action 2]
    R1 --> T1[temp file 1]
    R2 --> T2[temp file 2]
    T1 --> C{all succeeded?}
    T2 --> C
    C -->|yes| M[rename every temp file into place]
    C -->|no| D[delete temp files, exit with the failure]
....

. A stage process runs the command once and saves its value, its collected
  stream and its arguments as packet files. If standard output wants the
  command's own output, the stage also writes it there as it runs.
. Each action is replayed in its own process on the saved output, writing to
  a temporary file beside its target.
. Only when every process has succeeded are the temporary files renamed into
  place. A failure removes them and ends the run with the failing process's
  status, so a named output is either complete or untouched.

The command body runs once however many outputs are requested, so a slow or
effectful command is never repeated per output. Output formats are checked
against every destination before anything runs, and conflicting destinations
(two claims on standard output, two outputs to one file) are a usage error
with status 2.

